content.text:
**codex-root tagged rc.2 at 14:57 (on main `19d6f0a`), and it still requires Node 20.** Its CI on main passed, and the release workflow builds the rc.2 package next.

Under your no-deprecated-packages rule, rc.2 can't become v1.0.0, because the stable release may differ from the RC only in version numbers. So you have two options:

1. **Keep the rule (my recommendation, since it's your stated rule):** tell codex-root to stop all qualification work on rc.2, so the held-out runs, installed-package checks and your manual terminal checks aren't spent on a dead candidate. Then:
   - update DEC-12 to Node 22 or newer;
   - change the branch rules from "test-matrix (20)" to (22) and (24);
   - get PR #137 reviewed and merged;
   - cut rc.3.
2. **Accept Node 20 for v1.0.0:** rc.2 continues as is, I close #137, and the Node 22 change moves to the next release.

On my side, I removed my edit to the rc.2 release notes, since those notes have to describe the rc.2 build as tagged; the Node 22 wording goes into the rc.3 notes instead. #137 is rebased onto the rc.2 commit locally (`6a62cd6`). I'll push it once the shared CI slot is free.

Next check is around 15:31.

